## Slice 2: Video Core (Upload / Feed / Search / Watch)

### 8. `video.routes.js` had the same blanket-auth issue as before

**Symptom:** N/A yet (caught during initial review, same as Slice 1 bug #2, since this file was scaffolded from the same course template).

**Root cause:** `router.use(verifyJWT)` applied globally, blocking public video browsing.

**Fix:** Applied `verifyJWT` per-route, only on `POST /`, `PATCH /:videoId`, `DELETE /:videoId`, and `PATCH /toggle/publish/:videoId`. `GET /` and `GET /:videoId` stayed public.

**File:** `video.routes.js`

---

### 9. `TypeError: Cannot destructure property 'title' of 'req.body' as it is undefined` in `updateVideo`

**Symptom:** Sending a `PATCH` request to update a video with a completely empty `multipart/form-data` body returned a raw `500` instead of the expected clean `400` validation error.

**Root cause:** Same category of bug as Slice 1's `refreshToken` crash — code assumed `req.body` would always be at least `{}`. When multer parses a genuinely empty multipart body, `req.body` comes back `undefined` rather than an empty object, so `const { title, description } = req.body` threw before any of the function's own validation logic could run.

**Fix:**
```js
const { title, description } = req.body || {}
```
Applied to both `updateVideo` and defensively to `publishAVideo` for consistency.

**File:** `video.controller.js` — `updateVideo`, `publishAVideo`

**Lesson (recurring):** This is the third time this exact pattern has appeared (`req.cookies` in Slice 1, now `req.body` here). Any destructuring of an Express request property (`req.body`, `req.cookies`, `req.params`, `req.query`) should default to `|| {}` unless there's a guarantee upstream (e.g. required middleware) that it will always be populated.

---

### 10. Autoplay-next feature looped between only 2 videos instead of advancing through the full list

**Symptom:** After the first video finished and auto-advanced to a second video, it would then loop back to the first video indefinitely (A → B → A → B), even with 4+ videos in the database.

**Root cause:** The "related videos" list on the Watch page was being fetched once on initial mount and not refreshed when navigating to a new video via autoplay-next — so the "exclude current video" check was comparing against a stale video ID rather than whatever was actually playing at that moment.

**Fix:** Ensured the related-videos fetch and the "current video" reference both update whenever the video ID changes (not just on initial mount), so up-next selection always excludes the actual currently-playing video and can keep advancing through the available list.

**File:** Watch page component (frontend)

---

### 11. View count added — deliberate scope decision, not a bug

**What:** `getVideoById` was updated to atomically increment `views` on every fetch:
```js
const video = await Video.findByIdAndUpdate(
    videoId,
    { $inc: { views: 1 } },
    { returnDocument: "after" }
).populate("owner", "username fullName avatar")
```
Used `$inc` specifically instead of a read-then-write pattern to avoid a race condition if multiple requests hit the same video concurrently.

**Known limitation, accepted deliberately:** This increments on every single fetch — page refreshes, repeat visits, and autoplay-next navigations all count as a "view," with no deduplication window or minimum watch-time threshold. Real platforms have much more sophisticated view-counting logic; this is the intentionally simple, "correct-enough for this stage" version. Worth mentioning as a known tradeoff if asked in an interview, not something to over-engineer now.

**File:** `video.controller.js` — `getVideoById`

