### 15. Nested `<a>` tags in `VideoCard` broke navigation unpredictably

**Symptom:** Clicking a video card from the home feed sometimes led to a broken/stuck Watch page. React console warning: `<a> cannot be a descendant of <a>`.

**Root cause:** `VideoCard` was structured as an outer `<Link to="/watch/:id">` wrapping the whole card, with an inner `<Link to="/channel/:username">` for the channel avatar/name nested inside it. This is invalid HTML — anchors cannot contain anchors — and browsers handle the resulting malformed DOM unpredictably. The original implementation tried to work around the resulting double-navigation with `e.stopPropagation()` on the inner link's click handler, which suppressed one symptom (both links firing on click) but did nothing to fix the invalid nesting itself.

**Fix (root cause, not the stopPropagation patch):** Restructured `VideoCard` so no anchor contains another:
- Outer wrapper changed to a plain `<div>` (not a `Link`)
- Thumbnail area became its own standalone `<Link to="/watch/:videoId">`
- Title became a separate sibling `<Link to="/watch/:videoId">`
- Channel avatar/name became a separate sibling `<Link to="/channel/:username">`
- All `stopPropagation()` calls removed — no longer needed since there's no nesting to suppress
- Thumbnail link given `tabIndex={-1} aria-hidden="true"` so keyboard users land on the title link (which has a proper `aria-label`) instead of a redundant duplicate tab stop

**File:** `VideoCard.jsx`, `VideoCard.css` (frontend)

**Lesson:** A JS-level workaround (`stopPropagation`) can mask a symptom of invalid HTML structure without fixing the structure itself. When React logs a DOM-validity warning, treat it as a structural defect to fix, not a warning to silence.

