### 4. Registration succeeded on backend, but frontend showed "Network error: Cannot reach the server"

**Symptom:** User + Cloudinary avatar were both created successfully (visible in MongoDB Atlas and Cloudinary), but the frontend never received a response — showed a generic network error.

**Initial (wrong) hypothesis:** Suspected `nodemon` was restarting mid-request because multer writes/deletes files inside `public/temp/`, which nodemon might interpret as a source change. Checked terminal logs — no `[nodemon] restarting` message appeared, so this was ruled out.

**Actual root cause:** `CORS_ORIGIN` in `.env` was set to `http://localhost:5173`, but Vite was actually running on `http://localhost:5174` (port 5173 was already occupied by a leftover process). The origin mismatch caused the browser to block the response entirely under CORS policy — the request completed server-side, but the browser never handed the response back to the frontend JS.

**Fix:** Freed port 5173 (`netstat -ano | findstr :5173` → `taskkill /PID <pid> /F`) so Vite could consistently bind to the same port that `.env`'s `CORS_ORIGIN` expected. (Alternative fix would have been updating `CORS_ORIGIN` to match whatever port Vite actually used.)

**Lesson:** A backend fully completing its work is not proof that the frontend received anything — CORS failures happen entirely in the browser, after the server has already done its job.

---

### 5. `CORS_ORIGIN=*` in `.env`

**Symptom:** Would have caused the same class of failure as bug #4, but caught before it caused a live issue.

**Root cause:** `app.js` uses `credentials: true` in its CORS config (required for cookie-based auth), but the CORS spec explicitly forbids combining `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` with a wildcard `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. Browsers reject this combination outright.

**Fix:** Set `CORS_ORIGIN` to the exact frontend origin (`http://localhost:5173` in dev; will be the deployed Vercel URL in production).

**File:** `.env`

---

