### 14. Watch history never populated — `req.user` was always `undefined` on the video-watch route

**Symptom:** Added `$addToSet` logic to `getVideoById` to record watched videos to the user's `watchHistory`, but `GET /users/history` kept returning an empty array even while logged in with valid cookies.

**Root cause:** `GET /videos/:videoId` is intentionally public (no `verifyJWT`) so logged-out users can watch videos — but `verifyJWT` is the *only* middleware that ever sets `req.user`. With it absent from this route, `req.user` was `undefined` for every request, logged in or not, so the `if (req.user?._id)` guard around the watch-history write could never be true.

**Fix:** Added a new `verifyJWTOptional` middleware — checks for a token and attaches `req.user` if valid, but never blocks the request if the token is missing or invalid (unlike `verifyJWT`, which throws `401` on missing/invalid tokens):
```js
export const verifyJWTOptional = asyncHandler(async(req, _, next) => {
    try {
        const token = req.cookies?.accessToken || req.header("Authorization")?.replace("Bearer ", "")
        if (!token) { req.user = null; return next() }
        const decodedToken = jwt.verify(token, process.env.ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET)
        const user = await User.findById(decodedToken?._id).select("-password -refreshToken")
        req.user = user || null
        next()
    } catch (error) {
        req.user = null
        next()
    }
})
```
Applied to `GET /videos/:videoId` only — `getWatchHistory` itself correctly stays behind the strict `verifyJWT`, since history is private with no public-viewing use case.

**Files:** `auth.middleware.js` (new export), `video.routes.js`

**Lesson:** "Public route with optional personalization for logged-in users" is a distinct auth pattern from both "fully public" and "fully protected" — it needs its own middleware, not a workaround bolted onto either extreme. Good one to be able to explain in interviews.

