### 19. A confusing, traceless `500` — a syntax typo was masking a real, fixable error underneath

**Symptom:** `POST /comments/:videoId` (`addComment`) consistently returned a generic `500 Internal Server Error`, but — highly unusually — **nothing printed in the backend terminal at all**, even across multiple full server restarts and after ruling out a zombie process on port 8000 (`netstat` showed only one listener).

**Investigation:** Since the usual approach (check the terminal trace) produced nothing, added an unmissable raw `console.log("!!! addComment was called !!!", ...)` as the very first line of the function to establish whether the request was even reaching the controller at all.

**Root cause, part 1:** A stray typo — `if (!content?.trim()) {p` — an extra `p` character sitting right after the opening brace. This is a genuine JavaScript syntax error, which broke module parsing in a way that didn't produce a normal, traceable stack trace the way a runtime error would.

**Root cause, part 2 (revealed only after fixing the typo):** Once the syntax error was fixed and the debug log successfully appeared, the *real* underlying bug became visible: `comment.controller.js` imported `mongoose` but not `isValidObjectId` specifically —
```js
import mongoose from "mongoose"   // missing { isValidObjectId }
```
— so calling `isValidObjectId(videoId)` threw `ReferenceError: isValidObjectId is not defined`.

**Fix:**
```js
import mongoose, {isValidObjectId} from "mongoose"
```
Matching the import pattern already used correctly in `video.controller.js` and `subscription.controller.js`.

**File:** `comment.controller.js`

**Lesson:** Not every bug leaves a helpful trace. A syntax error can break module loading in ways that produce confusing, silent failures instead of a clean crash — when a normally-reliable debugging method (checking the terminal trace) suddenly produces nothing, the next step is to add an unmistakable, impossible-to-miss log line to establish ground truth about whether code is executing at all, rather than continuing to guess at runtime causes for a problem that was actually happening at parse time.

