### 22. Feature addition: video owners can delete (not edit) any comment on their own video

**What:** Extended `deleteComment` so that in addition to a comment's author being able to delete their own comment, the *video's owner* can also delete any comment on their own video — standard moderation capability on real video platforms. Editing intentionally stays restricted to the comment's author only; only deletion was extended.

**Backend change:**
```js
const video = await Video.findById(comment.video)
const isCommentOwner = comment.owner.toString() === req.user._id.toString()
const isVideoOwner = video?.owner?.toString() === req.user._id.toString()

if (!isCommentOwner && !isVideoOwner) {
    throw new ApiError(403, "You are not authorized to delete this comment")
}
```
Required importing `Video` into `comment.controller.js`.

**Frontend change:** `CommentItem`'s delete-option visibility condition extended to show if the current user is either the comment's author OR the video's owner (video owner id passed down from the Watch page, where it's already available from the video fetch).

**Files:** `comment.controller.js` (backend), `CommentItem.jsx` / `CommentSection.jsx` (frontend)

**Lesson:** This was a deliberate feature addition, not a bug fix — a good example of recognizing a real platform pattern (two-tier comment permissions: author edit/delete vs. owner moderate-delete) and implementing it as a small, clean extension of existing authorization logic rather than a parallel system.

