# Yeonix — Bugs Journal

A running log of real bugs hit during development, their root causes, and how they were fixed. Kept for interview prep and future debugging reference — the goal is to remember *why* something broke, not just that it did.

---

## Slice 1: Authentication (Register / Login / Logout / Refresh)

### 1. Cookies not persisting after login (`secure: true` hardcoded)

**Symptom:** Login appeared to succeed in the network tab, but the session didn't persist — `getCurrentUser` would fail right after.

**Root cause:** `loginUser` and `logoutUser` had `secure: true` hardcoded in the cookie options. Browsers refuse to store `secure` cookies over plain `http://localhost` (no TLS), so the `accessToken`/`refreshToken` cookies were silently dropped. `refreshAccessToken` already handled this correctly with an environment check — the other two functions just hadn't been updated to match.

**Fix:** Made cookie options environment-aware in all three functions:
```js
const options = {
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
    sameSite: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production" ? "none" : "lax"
}
```
Also required adding `NODE_ENV=development` to `.env`, since it wasn't set at all — meant `process.env.NODE_ENV` was `undefined`, which happened to evaluate safely but wasn't reliable long-term.

**File:** `user.controller.js` — `loginUser`, `logoutUser`

---

### 2. `video.routes.js` blocked public video browsing

**Symptom:** N/A yet (caught during code review, before it caused a live bug).

**Root cause:** `router.use(verifyJWT)` was applied globally at the top of the file, forcing auth on every video route — including `GET /` (list videos) and `GET /:videoId` (view a single video). A video platform needs logged-out users to browse/watch without an account.

**Fix:** Removed the blanket `router.use(verifyJWT)` and applied `verifyJWT` per-route, only on routes that mutate data (create, update, delete, toggle-publish).

**File:** `video.routes.js`

---

### 3. Missing global error-handling middleware

**Symptom:** N/A yet (caught during code review).

**Root cause:** `asyncHandler` forwards errors via `next(err)`, but `app.js` had no error-handling middleware registered to catch them — so thrown `ApiError`s would fall through to Express's default HTML error page instead of returning the app's structured JSON (`{success, message, errors, data}`). This would have broken every frontend error-handling call (`err.response?.data?.message`).

**Fix:** Added a centralized error handler at the end of `app.js`, after all routes, that checks `instanceof ApiError` and returns consistent JSON; falls back to a generic 500 for unexpected errors.

**File:** `app.js`

---

### 4. Registration succeeded on backend, but frontend showed "Network error: Cannot reach the server"

**Symptom:** User + Cloudinary avatar were both created successfully (visible in MongoDB Atlas and Cloudinary), but the frontend never received a response — showed a generic network error.

**Initial (wrong) hypothesis:** Suspected `nodemon` was restarting mid-request because multer writes/deletes files inside `public/temp/`, which nodemon might interpret as a source change. Checked terminal logs — no `[nodemon] restarting` message appeared, so this was ruled out.

**Actual root cause:** `CORS_ORIGIN` in `.env` was set to `http://localhost:5173`, but Vite was actually running on `http://localhost:5174` (port 5173 was already occupied by a leftover process). The origin mismatch caused the browser to block the response entirely under CORS policy — the request completed server-side, but the browser never handed the response back to the frontend JS.

**Fix:** Freed port 5173 (`netstat -ano | findstr :5173` → `taskkill /PID <pid> /F`) so Vite could consistently bind to the same port that `.env`'s `CORS_ORIGIN` expected. (Alternative fix would have been updating `CORS_ORIGIN` to match whatever port Vite actually used.)

**Lesson:** A backend fully completing its work is not proof that the frontend received anything — CORS failures happen entirely in the browser, after the server has already done its job.

---

### 5. `CORS_ORIGIN=*` in `.env`

**Symptom:** Would have caused the same class of failure as bug #4, but caught before it caused a live issue.

**Root cause:** `app.js` uses `credentials: true` in its CORS config (required for cookie-based auth), but the CORS spec explicitly forbids combining `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` with a wildcard `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. Browsers reject this combination outright.

**Fix:** Set `CORS_ORIGIN` to the exact frontend origin (`http://localhost:5173` in dev; will be the deployed Vercel URL in production).

**File:** `.env`

---

### 6. Login threw `500 Internal Server Error` — `bcrypt.compare` given `undefined`

**Symptom:** Registration worked, but logging in with valid credentials returned a `500`.

**Root cause:** Added `select: false` to the `password` field in `user.model.js` (correct hardening — prevents password hashes from leaking on normal queries), but didn't update `loginUser` to explicitly re-include the password field when looking up the user for authentication. `User.findOne(...)` returned a user object with `password` excluded, so `bcrypt.compare(password, undefined)` threw `Error: data and hash arguments required`.

**Fix:**
```js
const user = await User.findOne({
    $or: [{username}, {email}]
}).select("+password")
```

**File:** `user.controller.js` — `loginUser`

**Lesson:** Adding `select: false` to a schema field is a "spooky action at a distance" change — every existing query that relied on that field being present by default needs to be audited and updated.

---

### 7. `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'refreshToken')` in `refreshAccessToken`

**Symptom:** Backend terminal repeatedly crashed with this error, seemingly at random, even when nothing looked wrong in the UI.

**Investigation:** Ruled out several wrong leads before finding it:
- Not caused by `select: false` (different code path than bug #6)
- Not a zombie/duplicate process on port 8000 (`netstat -ano | findstr :8000` showed only one listener)
- Confirmed via temporary debug logging (`console.log(req.cookies)`, `console.log(req.headers.cookie)`) that `req.cookies` really was present as an object — just without an `accessToken`/`refreshToken` key, since the user was logged out

**Actual root cause — a deadlock in the frontend's axios interceptor, not a backend bug at all:**
1. `AuthContext` calls `getCurrentUser()` on every app load, even while logged out.
2. Backend correctly returns `401`.
3. The axios response interceptor catches *any* `401` and tries to silently refresh via `POST /users/refresh-token`.
4. Since the user was never logged in, there's no refresh token either — that call *also* returns `401`.
5. Because the refresh call was made through the same `axiosInstance`, it passed back through the same interceptor. The interceptor saw another `401`, and — since `isRefreshing` was already `true` — queued this request to wait for `processQueue()`.
6. But `processQueue()` was only ever going to be called by the *same* refresh flow that was now stuck waiting on this queued promise. It was waiting on itself. Permanent hang, no crash, no error — just infinite loading on the frontend.

**Fix (frontend):** Added a check in the interceptor to detect when the *failing* request is the refresh-token call itself, and short-circuit straight to "session expired" instead of attempting to queue/retry it:
```js
const isRefreshCall = originalRequest?.url?.includes('/users/refresh-token');

if (error.response?.status === 401 && isRefreshCall) {
    isRefreshing = false;
    processQueue(error);
    window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('yeonix:session-expired'));
    return Promise.reject(error);
}
```

**Fix (backend, defensive hardening — kept regardless of the frontend fix):**
```js
const incomingRefreshToken = req.cookies?.refreshToken || req.body?.refreshToken
```
A publicly-reachable endpoint (hit by every anonymous visitor on page load) should never assume `req.cookies` or `req.body` are defined — it should fail cleanly with a `401`, not crash.

**File:** `axiosInstance.js` (frontend), `user.controller.js` — `refreshAccessToken` (backend)

**Lesson:** A `401` from a *refresh* endpoint means "there is no session to refresh" — it's terminal, not retryable. Treating it the same as a `401` from any other endpoint creates a self-referential retry loop.

