### 17. `isSubscribed` always `false` on the Channel page/Watch page, even when actually subscribed

**Symptom:** After subscribing to a channel, navigating away and back showed the Subscribe button reverted to "Subscribe" instead of "Subscribed" — and clicking it again would incorrectly toggle the subscription off.

**Investigation:** Added temporary `console.log` debug lines through the full frontend chain (API response handler → state update → component prop) and confirmed the frontend was correctly receiving, storing, and passing along whatever the backend sent — the backend itself was returning `isSubscribed: false` unconditionally.

**Root cause:** When `GET /users/c/:username` was originally made public (Slice 3, bug #13), `verifyJWT` was simply removed with nothing put in its place — unlike the equivalent fix on the video route, which correctly used the new `verifyJWTOptional` middleware. With no auth middleware at all on this route, `req.user` was always `undefined`, for every request, logged in or not. The controller's `isSubscribed` computation (`{$in: [req.user?._id, "$subscribers.subscriber"]}`) could therefore never match, and always evaluated to `false` — for everyone, unconditionally.

**Fix:**
```js
router.route("/c/:username").get(verifyJWTOptional, getUserChannelProfile)
```
Same middleware already built for the video route in bug #14 — applied here too.

**File:** `user.routes.js`

**Lesson:** This is the second time the exact same category of bug appeared from the exact same root cause (a route made public by deleting `verifyJWT` outright, instead of swapping it for `verifyJWTOptional`). Any route that needs to be "public but personalized," now and in the future, must use `verifyJWTOptional` — never just remove auth entirely and assume `req.user` will be handled gracefully downstream.

